// The café's return visits – a sibling of cafe.json5 for the one lesson the café's own columns cannot
// teach: an event outcome compared between two groups, used by the NNT block of
// docs/en/concepts/effect-sizes.md. The café followed 400 first-time visitors – 200 in the four weeks
// before the menu change, 200 in the four weeks after – and noted whether each came back within a
// month. Generated by the exam builder's authored spec format (docs/en/exam-spec-reference.md):
//
//   app-gen --spec docs/examples/cafe-return.json5 --name cafe-return --authored
//
// The name is the seed, so `--name cafe-return` reproduces the dataset exactly; cafe-return.csv is
// that dataset, written as cafe.csv is. Every number the concept page quotes from it comes from that
// file, so a change to anything here – the name included – is a change to the prose.
// cafe-return.ru.csv is this dataset with its header (`меню`, `вернулся`) and menu labels («Новое меню»,
// «Старое меню») translated, every other value identical – the labels sort as the English ones do, so the
// Russian docs quote it with every signed difference unchanged.
//
// Structure: one between-subjects factor, `menu`, and one 0/1 outcome, `returned` (1 = came back).
// The outcome is a normal latent cut at a threshold, the old menu's latent mean 0.25 below the
// threshold's centre and the new menu's 0.25 above, so the two differ by d = 0.50 on the latent
// scale. The bernoulli parameter Φ(0.25) = 0.5987 puts the old menu's planted return rate at
// Φ(0) = 0.50 and the new menu's at Φ(0.50) = 0.6915 – Furukawa's NNT for d = 0.50 at a control
// event rate of 0.50, 1 ⁄ 0.1915 = 5.22, the analysis planner's own conversion. No missing data and
// no deliberate flaws.
{
	n: 400,
	design: {
		between: { menu: ["Old menu", "New menu"] },
		outcomes: {
			returned: { family: "bernoulli", params: [0.5987], effect: "menu*0.5" },
		},
	},
}
